\paragraph{Multicolored Graph Problems.}
\label{ssec:multicol}
In many cases, a vertex-colored version of the graph problems can help to construct diminishers. As an example, it remains open whether the problem \pprob{Path}{k}, asking whether a given graph contains a simple path of length $k$, is diminishable. However for \pprob{Multicolored Path}{k} we can show that it is.
The \prob{Multicolored Path} problem is defined as follows: Given an undirected graph~$G=(V,E)$ with a vertex coloring function $\col: V \to \{1,\ldots,k\}$, determine whether there exists a simple path of length~$k$ with one vertex of each color. This problem is \textnormal{NP}{}-complete as it generalizes \prob{Hamiltonian Path}. 
Furthermore, \pprob{Multicolored Path}{k} is in FPT as it can be solved in~$2^{O(k)} n^2$~time~\cite{AlonYZ95}.

